{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"46e71df83ac813417515677a514a7bbbf5fa06cdbc60a6585d79d3b4a3a494b0","slug":"spherex","location":"/tutorials/spherex/spherex.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"SPHEREx Tutorial Notebooks","content_includes_title":false,"license":{"content":{"id":"BSD-3-Clause","url":"https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/irsa-tutorials/blob/main/LICENSE","name":"BSD 3-Clause \"New\" or \"Revised\" License","free":true,"osi":true}},"github":"https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/irsa-tutorials/","subject":"IRSA Tutorials","keywords":["astronomy"],"copyright":"California Institute of Technology","settings":{"output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"source_url":"https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/irsa-tutorials//blob/main/tutorials/spherex/spherex.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/Caltech-IPAC/irsa-tutorials//edit/main/tutorials/spherex/spherex.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"spherex.md","url":"/irsa-tutorials/_public/spherex-bed284d784026f6c728e9e58c0107cef.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Missions/spherex.html","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"SPHEREx","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"c8ajdcFreJ"}],"urlSource":"https://irsa.ipac.caltech.edu/Missions/spherex.html","key":"m5lIPISsTN"},{"type":"text","value":" (Spectro-Photometer for the History of the Universe, Epoch of Reionization, and Ices Explorer) is a NASA space mission designed to perform the first all-sky spectral survey in the near-infrared.\nSPHEREx observes the sky from roughly 0.75–5.0 µm using a single instrument that provides low-resolution spectroscopy (R ≈ 40–150) in hundreds of spectral channels for every point on the sky.\nIts science goals span cosmology, galaxy evolution, and the interstellar medium, enabling measurements of large-scale structure, the cosmic history of star formation, and the distribution of key molecules and ices in the Milky Way and nearby galaxies.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"waWujE47Hk"}],"key":"BzmEwRXJGz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"SPHEREx data releases include weekly ","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"G5eQIQg6kj"},{"type":"link","url":"https://caltech-ipac.github.io/spherex-archive-documentation/spherex-data-products/","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Release spectral image products","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"gLkVWQlOab"}],"urlSource":"https://caltech-ipac.github.io/spherex-archive-documentation/spherex-data-products/","key":"eGWIfpUKe4"},{"type":"text","value":" as well as periodic Data Releases (e.g., DR1) (multi-extension FITS files containing calibrated near-infrared surface brightness, variance, flags, modeled backgrounds, PSFs, and wavelength WCS) along with ancillary calibration and metadata files such as gain matrices, dark current maps, solid angle pixel maps, and detailed spectral WCS products for each detector.\nQR3 and DR1 supersede QR2, and SPHEREx recommends using QR3 and DR1 over QR2.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"MXy3zzUMmD"}],"key":"knyqzq6Yzk"},{"type":"grid","columns":[1,2,2,3],"children":[{"type":"card","url":"/spherex-intro","children":[{"type":"header","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","url":"/spherex-intro","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data Overview →","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,